  "question": {
    "text": "<p>What happens to freezing point of benzene when small quantity of napthalene is added to benzene?  </p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "KpI8O",
      "text": "Increases",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "O39Rm",
      "text": "Remains unchanged ",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "9um3y",
      "text": "First decreases and then increases",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "JZsoU",
      "text": "Decreases",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Depression in Freezing point can be calculated as,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∆</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi></msub><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math></p><p>When a non-volatile component is added to the solvent, its freezing point decreases. Here, Napthalene is a non-volatile substance which decreases the freezing point of benzene.</p><p>On addition of naphthalene to benzene there is depression in freezing point of benzene.</p><p>Hence, the answer is option D.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
